|~ `::number` and what is declared under it: its constructor and the
   refusals of a payload of another kind and of an answer that leaves
   the doubles, and the words that run on a number.
   The decisions it exists for: [D114]. ~|

:number
  |~~ A number is a finite double, the one kind of number: a whole
      number and a decimal are one kind, `3 | eq 3.0` answering `true`,
      and a sum rounds as doubles round, `0.1 | add 0.2` answering
      `0.30000000000000004`. Infinity and NaN are no values, so a step
      whose answer leaves the range answers a failure.

      It is written as JSON writes it, `42`, `-1.5`, `2.5e-3`, and
      prints the shortest digits that read back, `1e21` printing
      `1e+21`; a literal past the range does not parse. `::number#5` is
      `5`, while text is never read as a number by its look:
      `::number"5"` is refused, and `toNumber` reads a number out of a
      string, `"42" | toNumber | add 1` answering `43`. Numbers rank
      after booleans and before strings.

      Its words add, subtract, multiply, divide and compare, `add`,
      `sub`, `mul`, `div`, `mod`, `gt`, and round, `round`, `floor`,
      `ceil`; `sum`, `avg`, `min` and `max` read the numbers of a
      vector, and `range` counts up to one.
      ::snippet~(::number | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::string/toNumber | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | wraps |~~ A number, read as itself, a finite double. ~~|
    | throws :PayloadNotNumberError
        |~~ `::number` reads its payload as the number it is; a payload of
            another kind is refused, and text is read by `toNumber`.
        ~~|
    | throws :NotFiniteError
        |~~ A number is a finite double: an answer brought under
            `::number` that left their range, a sum or a product too
            large, is refused here, whichever word computed it, and the
            trail names the step and the subject it ran on.
        ~~|
    | proves :isANumber |~~ Five is a number. ~~| ~(5 | type | eq ::number)
    | proves :refusesALiteralPastTheRange
        |~~ The literal 1e400 does not parse. ~~|
        ~("1e400" | parse !| type | eq ::quote/ParseError)
    | proves :answersAnErrorPastTheRange
        |~~ A product past the range answers an error. ~~|
        ~(1e308 | mul 10 !| type | eq ::number/NotFiniteError)
    | proves :roundsAsADouble
        |~~ A tenth and two tenths add as doubles add. ~~|
        ~(get [-1 2.5e-3 ~(0.1 | add 0.2)] | eq [-1 0.0025 0.30000000000000004])
    | proves :PayloadNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string payload is refused; `toNumber` reads a number from
            text.
        ~~|
        ~(::number"5" !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/PayloadNotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :NotFiniteError/refusesAProductPastTheRange
        |~~ A product past the range of doubles is refused at the step
            that made it.
        ~~|
        ~(1e308 | mul 10 | add 1 !| get [~(type) ~(get /trail | map /step)]
          | eq [::number/NotFiniteError [~(mul 10)]])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/add
  |~~ Answers the subject plus the number the call gives, `10 | add 3`.
      The subject is a number: a vector adds to each of its elements by
      `map`, `map ~(add 1)`, and sums them by `sum`; a string joins by
      `append`; and text that holds a number is read first by
      `toNumber`, `"5" | toNumber | add 1`. The addend is computed
      against the subject, so `5 | add (mul 2)` adds ten, and two fields
      of one record add by `sumOf /x /y`. A number under a tag answers
      under the tag, `::Width#2 | add 3` answering `::Width#5`, and a
      sum past the doubles is refused as no number.
      ::link~(::number/sub | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :addend
        |~~ The number added to the subject, computed against the subject.
        ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :addend/NotNumberError
        |~~ The addend is a number; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The subject and the addend added, under the tags of the
            subject.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `add` adds to a number: two fields of one record add by
            `sumOf /x /y`, a vector adds to each element by `map`,
            `map ~(add 1)`, a string joins by `append`, and text that
            holds a number is read first by `toNumber`.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `add` adds one number to the subject: the numbers of a vector
            sum by `sum`, `[1 2 3] | sum`, and two fields of one record
            add by `sumOf /x /y`.
        ~~|
    | proves :addsTheNumber
        |~~ `add` answers the sum. ~~|
        ~(10 | add 3 | eq 13)
    | proves :computesTheAddendAgainstTheSubject
        |~~ The addend is computed against the subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | add (mul 2) | eq 15)
    | proves :refusesASumPastTheDoubles
        |~~ A sum past the doubles is refused as no number. ~~|
        ~(1e308 | add 1e308 !| type | eq ::number/NotFiniteError)
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~("hello" | add 1 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :addend/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ An addend that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(1 | add "x" !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/add/addend/NotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoNumbers
        |~~ Args of two numbers are refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(1 | add 1 2 !| type | eq ::number/add/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :answersUnderTheTag
        |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Width#2 | add 3 | eq ::Width#5)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/sub
  |~~ Answers the subject, the minuend, less the number the call gives,
      `10 | sub 3`, which may fall below zero, `5 | sub 7` answering
      `-2`. The subject is a number: a vector subtracts from each of its
      elements by `map`, `map ~(sub 1)`, and text that holds a number is
      read first by `toNumber`. The subtrahend is computed against the
      subject, and two fields of one record subtract by
      `subOf /total /paid`. A number under a tag answers under the tag.
      ::link~(::number/add | doc) ::link~(::any/subOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :subtrahend
        |~~ The number subtracted from the subject, computed against the
            subject.
        ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :subtrahend/NotNumberError
        |~~ The subtrahend is a number; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `10 | sub ("3" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The subject less the subtrahend, under the tags of the
            subject.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `sub` subtracts from a number: two fields of one record
            subtract by `subOf /total /paid`, a vector subtracts from each
            element by `map`, `map ~(sub 1)`, and text that holds a number
            is read first by `toNumber`.
        ~~|
    | proves :subtractsTheNumber
        |~~ `sub` answers the difference. ~~|
        ~(10 | sub 3 | eq 7)
    | proves :fallsBelowZero
        |~~ A difference may fall below zero. ~~|
        ~(5 | sub 7 | eq -2)
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~("hello" | sub 1 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :subtrahend/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subtrahend that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(1 | sub "x" !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/sub/subtrahend/NotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :answersUnderTheTag
        |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Width#5 | sub 3 | eq ::Width#2)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/mul
  |~~ Answers the subject times the number the call gives, `10 | mul 3`,
      a fraction among them, `7 | mul 0.5` answering `3.5`. The subject
      is a number: a vector multiplies each of its elements by `map`,
      `map ~(mul 2)`, and text that holds a number is read first by
      `toNumber`. The multiplier is computed against the subject, so
      `5 | mul it` squares it, and two fields of one record multiply by
      `mulOf /price /qty`. A number under a tag answers under the tag,
      and a product past the doubles is refused as no number.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc) ::link~(::any/mulOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :multiplier
        |~~ The number the subject is multiplied by, computed against the
            subject.
        ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :multiplier/NotNumberError
        |~~ The multiplier is a number; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `2 | mul ("3" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The subject times the multiplier, under the tags of the
            subject.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `mul` multiplies a number: two fields of one record multiply
            by `mulOf /price /qty`, a vector multiplies each element by
            `map`, `map ~(mul 2)`, and text that holds a number is read
            first by `toNumber`.
        ~~|
    | proves :multipliesByTheNumber
        |~~ `mul` answers the product. ~~|
        ~(10 | mul 3 | eq 30)
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~("hello" | mul 2 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :multiplier/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A multiplier that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(1 | mul "x" !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/mul/multiplier/NotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :multiplier/NotNumberError/namesTheReadOfAPath
        |~~ A path given for the multiplier names the read of its field. ~~|
        ~(5 | mul /qty !| get /read | eq ~(mul (get /qty)))
    | proves :answersUnderTheTag
        |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Width#2 | mul 3 | eq ::Width#6)
    | proves :multipliesByAFraction
        |~~ A fraction multiplies. ~~|
        ~(7 | mul 0.5 | eq 3.5)
    | proves :computesTheMultiplierAgainstTheSubject
        |~~ The multiplier is computed against the subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | mul it | eq 25)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/div
  |~~ Answers the subject, the dividend, divided by the number the call
      gives, a fraction where the division leaves one, `7 | div 2`
      answering `3.5`. A divisor of zero is refused, so a divisor that
      may be zero is tested first,
      `if (get /count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(divOf /sum /count)`. The subject is a
      number: a vector divides each of its elements by `map`,
      `map ~(div 2)`. The divisor is computed against the subject, and
      two fields of one record divide by `divOf /sum /count`. A number
      under a tag answers under the tag.
      ::link~(::number/mul | doc) ::link~(::any/divOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :divisor
        |~~ The number the subject is divided by, computed against the
            subject.
        ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :divisor/NotNumberError
        |~~ The divisor is a number; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `6 | div ("3" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | holds :divisor/ZeroError
        |~~ The divisor is not zero: a division by zero has no finite
            answer, and a divisor that may be zero is tested before the
            call, as a record that counts its sum tests its count, which
            answers `0`:
            `{:sum 9 :count 0} | if (get /count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(divOf /sum /count)`.
        ~~|
        ~(ne 0)
    | returns
        |~~ The subject divided by the divisor, under the tags of the
            subject.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `div` divides a number: two fields of one record divide by
            `divOf /sum /count`, a vector divides each element by `map`,
            `map ~(div 2)`, and text that holds a number is read first by
            `toNumber`.
        ~~|
    | proves :dividesByTheNumber
        |~~ `div` answers the quotient. ~~|
        ~(10 | div 2 | eq 5)
    | proves :leavesAFraction
        |~~ A division that leaves a fraction answers it. ~~|
        ~(7 | div 2 | eq 3.5)
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~("hello" | div 2 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/div/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :divisor/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A divisor that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(1 | div "x" !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/div/divisor/NotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :divisor/ZeroError/refusesZero
        |~~ A division by zero is refused under the divisor. ~~|
        ~(10 | div 0 !| type | eq ::number/div/divisor/ZeroError)
    | proves :answersUnderTheTag
        |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Width#6 | div 3 | eq ::Width#2)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/mod
  |~~ Answers the remainder of the subject divided by the number the
      call gives, its sign the divisor's: `7 | mod 3` answers `1` and
      `-7 | mod 3` answers `2`, so `mod 2` tells an even number from an
      odd one, and a fraction leaves its own, `7.5 | mod 2` answering
      `1.5`. The divisor is computed against the subject, and a number
      under a tag answers under the tag.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :divisor
        |~~ The number the subject is divided by, computed against the
            subject.
        ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :divisor/NotNumberError
        |~~ The divisor is a number; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `7 | mod ("3" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | holds :divisor/ZeroError
        |~~ The divisor is not zero: a division by zero leaves no
            remainder, and a value that may be zero is asked first,
            `if (eq 0) …`.
        ~~|
        ~(ne 0)
    | returns
        |~~ The remainder, its sign the divisor's, under the tags of the
            subject.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `mod` divides a number: text that holds one is read first by
            `toNumber`, `"7" | toNumber | mod 3`.
        ~~|
    | proves :leavesTheRemainder
        |~~ `mod` answers what the division leaves. ~~|
        ~(7 | mod 3 | eq 1)
    | proves :takesTheSignOfTheDivisor
        |~~ The remainder takes the sign of the divisor. ~~|
        ~(get [~(-7 | mod 3) ~(7 | mod -3)] | eq [2 -2])
    | proves :tellsAnEvenNumber
        |~~ `mod 2` answers zero for an even number. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3 4] | filter ~(mod 2 | eq 0) | eq [2 4])
    | proves :leavesAFraction
        |~~ A fraction leaves a fraction. ~~|
        ~(7.5 | mod 2 | eq 1.5)
    | proves :answersUnderTheTag
        |~~ A number under a tag answers under the tag. ~~|
        ~(::Width#7 | mod 3 | eq ::Width#1)
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~("7" | mod 3 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/mod/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :divisor/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A divisor that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(7 | mod "3" !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/mod/divisor/NotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :divisor/ZeroError/refusesZero
        |~~ A remainder of a division by zero is refused under the
            divisor.
        ~~|
        ~(7 | mod 0 !| type | eq ::number/mod/divisor/ZeroError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/abs
  |~~ Answers the subject without its sign, `-3 | abs` answering `3`, a
      number under a tag under the tag. It takes no value.
      ::link~(::number/sub | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The subject without its sign, under the tags of the subject. ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `abs` takes the sign off a number: text that holds one is read
            first by `toNumber`, `"-3" | toNumber | abs`.
        ~~|
    | proves :dropsTheSign
        |~~ A negative number loses its sign, and a positive one stays. ~~|
        ~([-3 3 -0.5] | map ~abs | eq [3 3 0.5])
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~("-3" | abs !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/abs/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/floor
  |~~ Answers the greatest whole number not above the subject,
      `2.7 | floor` answering `2` and `-2.1 | floor` answering `-3`;
      `round` answers the nearest and `ceil` the least not below. It
      takes no value.
      ::link~(::number/ceil | doc) ::link~(::number/round | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The greatest whole number not above the subject, under its
            tags.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `floor` rounds a number down: text that holds one is read
            first by `toNumber`, `"2.7" | toNumber | floor`.
        ~~|
    | proves :roundsDown
        |~~ A number rounds down, a negative one away from zero. ~~|
        ~([2.7 -2.1 3] | map ~floor | eq [2 -3 3])
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~("2.7" | floor !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/floor/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/ceil
  |~~ Answers the least whole number not below the subject, `2.1 | ceil`
      answering `3` and `-2.7 | ceil` answering `-2`; `floor` answers
      the greatest not above. It takes no value.
      ::link~(::number/floor | doc) ::link~(::number/round | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The least whole number not below the subject, under its tags.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `ceil` rounds a number up: text that holds one is read first
            by `toNumber`, `"2.1" | toNumber | ceil`.
        ~~|
    | proves :roundsUp
        |~~ A number rounds up, a negative one toward zero, and prints no
            negative zero.
        ~~|
        ~([2.1 -2.7 3 -0.5] | map ~ceil | eq [3 -2 3 0])
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subject that is no number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~("2.1" | ceil !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/ceil/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/range
  |~~ Answers the whole numbers from zero up to the subject, the subject
      left out: `5 | range` answers `[0 1 2 3 4]`, and zero or below
      answers `[]`. A count from one is `range | map ~(add 1)`, and the
      vector reads as any other, `5 | range | map ~(mul 2)`. The subject
      is a whole number. It takes no value.
      ::link~(::vec/map | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The whole numbers from zero below the subject. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :CountNotIntegerError
        |~~ The count of `range`, its subject, is a whole number: a
            fraction is rounded first, `7.5 | floor | range`.
        ~~|
    | throws :CountTooLargeError
        |~~ The count of `range` is no longer than the length a vector of
            the host may have, which `:longest` names: a count past it
            makes no vector, and the call has no repair but a count within
            it.
        ~~|
    | carries :CountTooLargeError/longest
        |~~ The length a vector of the host may have, the count past which
            `range` makes none.
        ~~|
        ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `range` counts up to a number: the count of a vector is read
            first, `[:a :b] | count | range`.
        ~~|
    | proves :countsFromZero
        |~~ The numbers start at zero and leave the subject out. ~~|
        ~(5 | range | eq [0 1 2 3 4])
    | proves :answersNothingBelowOne
        |~~ Zero and below answer the empty vector. ~~|
        ~([0 -2] | map ~range | eq [[] []])
    | proves :countsFromOneByMap
        |~~ A count from one adds one to each. ~~|
        ~(3 | range | map ~(add 1) | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :CountNotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
        |~~ A fraction is no count. ~~|
        ~(2.5 | range !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/range/CountNotIntegerError ::number])
    | proves :CountTooLargeError/refusesACountPastTheLongest
        |~~ A count past the length `:longest` names is refused. ~~|
        ~(1e10 | range !| get [~(type) ~(get /longest | lt 1e10)] | eq [::number/range/CountTooLargeError true])
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAVector
        |~~ A vector is counted first. ~~|
        ~([:a :b] | range !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/range/SubjectNotNumberError ::vec])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/round
  |~~ Answers the subject rounded to the digits after the point the call
      gives, none when it gives none: `3.14159 | round 2` answers `3.14`
      and `2.5 | round` answers `3`. A half rounds away from zero, as
      the digits are written, `1.005 | round 2` answering `1.01` and
      `-2.5 | round` answering `-3`; a negative count rounds to tens and
      beyond, `1234 | round -2` answering `1200`; and a number rounded
      to zero prints as `0`. The digits are a whole number. The subject
      is a number, and text that holds one is read first by `toNumber`.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :digits
        |~~ The digits after the point, a whole number, none when the call
            gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0 0) ::number
    | throws :digits/NotNumberError
        |~~ The count of digits is a number, `3.14159 | round 2`. ~~|
    | holds :digits/NotIntegerError
        |~~ The digits of `round` are a whole number, `round 2`. ~~|
        ~(eq (floor))
    | returns |~~ The subject rounded to the digits. ~~| ::number
    | throws :SubjectNotNumberError
        |~~ `round` rounds a number: text that holds one is read first by
            `toNumber`, `"2.5" | toNumber | round`.
        ~~|
    | proves :keepsTheDigitsGiven
        |~~ Two digits keep two places after the point. ~~|
        ~(3.14159 | round 2 | eq 3.14)
    | proves :roundsAHalfAwayFromZero
        |~~ A half rounds away from zero. ~~|
        ~(2.5 | round | eq 3)
    | proves :roundsANegativeHalfAwayFromZero
        |~~ A negative half rounds away from zero too. ~~|
        ~(-2.5 | round | eq -3)
    | proves :roundsTheDigitsAsWritten
        |~~ A half rounds as the digits are written. ~~|
        ~(1.005 | round 2 | eq 1.01)
    | proves :roundsToHundreds
        |~~ A negative count rounds to tens and beyond. ~~|
        ~(1234 | round -2 | eq 1200)
    | proves :printsNoNegativeZero
        |~~ A negative number rounded to zero prints as 0. ~~|
        ~(-0.4 | round | print | eq "0")
    | proves :SubjectNotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no number to round. ~~|
        ~("2.5" | round !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/round/SubjectNotNumberError ::string])
    | proves :digits/NotIntegerError/refusesAFraction
        |~~ Half a digit is refused. ~~|
        ~(2.5 | round 0.5 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::number/round/digits/NotIntegerError ::number])
    | proves :digits/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A count of digits that is no number is refused under the
            input.
        ~~|
        ~(3.14 | round "2" !| type | eq ::number/round/digits/NotNumberError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/gt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is greater than the number the call
      gives, `10 | gt 5`, a decimal against a whole number among them,
      `3 | gt 2.5`. Numbers compare with numbers: a string, a keyword, a
      tag name and a value under `::desc` each compare with their own
      kind by a `gt` of their own, and a vector, a set, a map or null
      answers no `gt`, which the refusal of the call names with the
      kinds that do. The value is computed against the subject, and in a
      condition the subject is the element, `filter ~(gt 1)`. `gte`
      answers whether the subject is greater or equal, `lt` and `lte`
      the reverse.
      ::link~(::number/gte | doc) ::link~(::number/lt | doc)
      ::link~(::number/lte | doc) ::link~(::desc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the
            subject, `5 | gt 3`.
        ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :value/NotNumberError
        |~~ The value compared is a number; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `5 | gt ("3" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :comparesNumbers |~~ Ten is greater than five. ~~| ~(10 | gt 5)
    | proves :comparesADecimal
        |~~ A whole number compares with a decimal. ~~|
        ~(3 | gt 2.5)
    | proves :keepsOneNumberApartFromItself
        |~~ A number is not greater than itself. ~~|
        ~(5 | gt 5 | not)
    | proves :filtersByTheElement
        |~~ In a condition the subject is the element. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1) | eq [2 3])
    | proves :value/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
        ~(5 | gt "a" !| type | eq ::number/gt/value/NotNumberError)
    | proves :namesTheKindsThatAnswerIt
        |~~ A vector answers no `gt`, and the refusal names the kinds that
            do.
        ~~|
        ~([1] | gt [0] !| get /addresses | eq #[::desc/gt ::keyword/gt ::number/gt ::string/gt ::tag/gt])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/gte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is greater than or equal to the number
      the call gives, `10 | gte 10`. It compares as `gt` does, numbers
      with numbers, a whole number with a decimal, `5 | gte 5.0`
      answering `true`.
      ::link~(::number/gt | doc) ::link~(::number/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the
            subject, `5 | gte 3`.
        ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :value/NotNumberError
        |~~ The value compared is a number; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `5 | gte ("3" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject comes after the value or equals it,
            `false` otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :holdsOfItself
        |~~ A number is greater than or equal to itself. ~~|
        ~(10 | gte 10)
    | proves :holdsOfItsDecimal
        |~~ A whole number equals its decimal. ~~|
        ~(5 | gte 5.0)
    | proves :value/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
        ~(5 | gte "3" !| type | eq ::number/gte/value/NotNumberError)
    | proves :failsBelow
        |~~ A smaller number is not at least the value. ~~|
        ~(2 | gte 3 | not)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/lt
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is less than the number the call gives,
      `5 | lt 10`. It compares as `gt` does, numbers with numbers, and
      `lte` answers whether the subject is less or equal.
      ::link~(::number/gt | doc) ::link~(::number/lte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the
            subject, `5 | lt 3`.
        ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :value/NotNumberError
        |~~ The value compared is a number; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `5 | lt ("3" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :comparesNumbers |~~ Five is less than ten. ~~| ~(5 | lt 10)
    | proves :keepsOneNumberApartFromItself
        |~~ A number is not less than itself. ~~|
        ~(5 | lt 5 | not)
    | proves :value/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
        ~(5 | lt "3" !| type | eq ::number/lt/value/NotNumberError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/lte
  |~~ Answers whether the subject is less than or equal to the number the
      call gives, `10 | lte 10`. It compares as `gt` does, numbers with
      numbers.
      ::link~(::number/lt | doc) ::link~(::number/gte | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The number the subject is compared with, computed against the
            subject, `5 | lte 3`.
        ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :value/NotNumberError
        |~~ The value compared is a number; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `5 | lte ("3" | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject comes before the value or equals it,
            `false` otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :holdsOfItself
        |~~ A number is less than or equal to itself. ~~|
        ~(10 | lte 10)
    | proves :value/NotNumberError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no number to compare with. ~~|
        ~(5 | lte "3" !| type | eq ::number/lte/value/NotNumberError)
    | proves :failsAbove
        |~~ A greater number is not at most the value. ~~|
        ~(2 | lte 1 | not)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/toString
  |~~ Answers the number written as text, as a query and JSON write it,
      `42 | toString` answering `"42"` and `0.5` answering `"0.5"`, so a
      number joins text, `get [~(5 | toString) "px"] | join ""`. `toNumber`
      turns the text back into the number.
      ::link~(::string/toNumber | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The text that writes the number. ~~| ::string
    | proves :writesTheNumber
        |~~ A number answers the text that writes it. ~~|
        ~([42 -1.5 1e21] | map ~toString | eq ["42" "-1.5" "1e+21"])
    | proves :readsBack
        |~~ A number turned into its string turns back into itself. ~~|
        ~(0.1 | add 0.2 | :n it | toString | toNumber | eq n)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/toKeyword
  |~~ Answers the keyword a number files under as a key, `1 | toKeyword`
      answering `:"1"`, as `groupBy` and `indexBy` file it, so a record a
      number names is read from the map they build,
      `byId | query (get /uid | toKeyword)`. `toNumber` turns the keyword back
      into the number.
      ::link~(::keyword/toNumber | doc) ::link~(::vec/indexBy | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The keyword of the number's text. ~~| ::keyword
    | proves :readsTheKeyIndexByFiledItUnder
        |~~ A number turns into the key `indexBy` files it under. ~~|
        ~([{:id 1 :n "Ann"}] | indexBy /id | query (1 | toKeyword) | get /n | eq "Ann")
    | proves :readsBack
        |~~ A number turned into its keyword turns back into itself. ~~|
        ~(1 | toKeyword | toNumber | eq 1)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:number/toNumber
  |~~ Answers the number itself, so `map ~toNumber` turns numbers and the
      strings that write numbers into numbers, `[1 "2"] | map ~toNumber`
      answering `[1 2]`.
      ::link~(::string/toNumber | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The number itself. ~~| ::number
    | proves :answersItself
        |~~ A number answers itself, so numbers and their strings turn
            into numbers.
        ~~|
        ~([1 "2"] | map ~toNumber | eq [1 2])
    | impl ::builtin{}

